{
  "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
  "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BLANCHARD: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The dual banking system of State and Federal regulation in our nation which we've enjoyed for over 140 years is one of the finest examples of cooperative federalism in our history. For 35 years, the States, not the OCC, have prudently exercised their authority over non-bank State-chartered operating local subsidiaries of national banks. Indeed, respondent Wachovia Mortgage complied with Michigan law for 6 years until in 2003 there was a corporate reshuffling and now it claims it's exempt from the same Michigan laws it complied with. The OCC through its Regulation 7.40006 has disrupted the careful balance and seeks to deprive the States of the regulatory authority that they have historically exercised."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: This still goes to the same thing. Does Michigan do this by grace? If Michigan wanted to, could it just tell the feds to butt out and say, you know, all these years we've been letting you come in first, and we only step in when we think you haven't done a good job, but we've had it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But -- but the last part of the reg says that what applies, no State law applies to a national bank operating subsidiary if it doesn't apply to the parent bank. And I began to think it fields preemption. Am I right in thinking that it is conflict preemption, not field, because it is conflict in the case of a national bank?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: But you do take a field preemption position, don't you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. LONG",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LONG: I think our position is there are multiple roots that we can win this case. One is based on conflict preemption. Another --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What is a functionally regulated subsidiary of a depository institution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Yes, but it is true, is it not, that as long as this mortgage company was a subsidiary of a holding company rather than the bank itself, the State would have done the visiting, the visitorial power, whereas once they changed the Federal Government assumed that responsibility?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0007",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SRINIVASAN: No, not if you're talking about corporate powers, because those are governed by State law. What it could do, though, is regulate the subsidiary's conduct of the business of banking, because, after all, an operating subsidiary has one purpose and one purpose only, and that's to carry out functions that the national bank itself could perform."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4f1bb12224c2-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Practically, is there any difference between the way they operated when they were just a corporation as before, and now a parent and sub?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1342",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "4f1bb12224c2f72f9255b6072166f3f4a0e5ea8fd26a96cfd1a39e4fe913eea4",
